//? Title: U-statistics: Unbiased Estimation, Overlap and Two Kinds of Uncertainty
//? Subtitle: Symmetric kernels, Hoeffding projections, jackknife diagnostics, degeneracy and incomplete averages with datamunge's stats::UStatistic
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Suppose a population quantity is the expected value of a function of several independent
observations, such as half the squared difference of two measurements. We want to estimate
that quantity from one sample, use all its observations fairly, and quantify the uncertainty
without pretending that overlapping pairs are independent. A U-statistic averages a symmetric
kernel over distinct-index subsets. We derive its unbiasedness, its first-order projection
and the extra Monte Carlo variability introduced by sampling only some subsets. Three-point
calculations connect the kernel terms to sample variance and delete-one diagnostics. An exact
four-point sampling law shows why a degenerate kernel need not have a normal limit, or a
useful first-order standard error. A two-point population sweep checks both variance rates.
On pooled iris sepal lengths the complete variance is 0.685694 square centimetres and Gini
mean difference is 0.946192 centimetres. A repeated-seed check separates computational error
from sampling uncertainty: conditional RMSE falls from 0.288 with 10 draws to 0.0169 with 3000.
)

\toc

> The method

>> Symmetric kernels and the estimand

Let \(X_1,\ldots,X_n\) be independent observations from a common distribution \(F\).
A symmetric degree-\(m\) kernel \(h\) is unchanged by permuting its \(m\) arguments.
Its population target and complete U-statistic are

$$
\theta=E[h(X_1,\ldots,X_m)],\qquad
U_n=\binom nm^{-1}\sum_{1\leq i_1<\cdots<i_m\leq n}h(X_{i_1},\ldots,X_{i_m}).
$$
\alttext(Theta is the expectation of a kernel on m independent observations. U n averages that kernel over every unordered m-subset of n distinct observation indices.)

Every term has expectation \(\theta\), so integrability gives \(E[U_n]=\theta\).
With finite kernel variance there is also a simple variance argument for using all subsets. Start with the unbiased
estimator \(h(X_1,\ldots,X_m)\), randomly permute all \(n\) indices, then average over
the permutations conditional on the observed sample. The conditional average is \(U_n\);
the law of total variance shows that this symmetrisation cannot increase variance.
Broader minimum-variance claims require specifying the distribution family and the class of
unbiased competitors; unbiasedness alone does not establish universal efficiency.

The observations may have equal values; it is their *indices* that must be distinct within
a tuple. The library accepts a callback of type `SymmetricKernel`. The caller must supply
a deterministic, symmetric function of the configured degree. Symmetry is not tested.
The finite-input checks reject NaNs and infinities rather than silently dropping observations.

Three familiar choices are the mean kernel \(h(x)=x\), the variance kernel
\(h(x,y)=(x-y)^2/2\), and Gini's mean-difference kernel \(h(x,y)=|x-y|\).
The second targets population variance because independent copies satisfy
\(E[(X-Y)^2]=2\operatorname{Var}(X)\). The identity

$$
\sum_{i<j}(x_i-x_j)^2=n\sum_i(x_i-\bar x)^2
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
U_n\left[\frac{(x-y)^2}{2}\right]=\frac{\sum_i(x_i-\bar x)^2}{n-1}
$$
\alttext(The sum of squared differences over unordered pairs equals n times the centred sum of squares. Averaging half those differences over all pairs gives the sample variance with divisor n minus one.)

makes the usual unbiased sample variance a degree-two U-statistic. Averaging over all ordered
pairs including \(i=j\), a V-statistic, instead gives the variance with divisor \(n\).
Gini's mean difference has the original measurement units; variance has squared units.
Gini grows linearly with a single extreme observation, variance quadratically. Neither has
bounded outlier influence, so calling Gini automatically robust would be misleading.

>> Overlap, Hoeffding projections and the non-degenerate limit

There are \(\binom nm\) kernel terms, but they share observations. Dividing the variance
of one term by that count would generally miss their covariance. For degree two define

$$
h_1(x)=E[h(x,X')]-\theta,\qquad
h_2(x,y)=h(x,y)-\theta-h_1(x)-h_1(y).
$$
\alttext(The first projection is the conditional kernel expectation minus theta. The second canonical projection removes theta and both first-projection terms from the pair kernel.)

Then \(E[h_1(X)]=0\) and \(E[h_2(X,X')\mid X]=0\). Substitution gives

$$
U_n-\theta=\frac2n\sum_i h_1(X_i)+\binom n2^{-1}\sum_{i<j}h_2(X_i,X_j),
\qquad
\operatorname{Var}(U_n)=\frac{4\zeta_1}{n}+\frac{2\zeta_2}{n(n-1)},
$$
\alttext(The centred degree-two U-statistic is twice the sample mean of the first projection plus the average of the canonical pair projection. Its exact variance is four zeta one over n plus two zeta two over n times n minus one.)

where \(\zeta_1=E[h_1(X)^2]\) and \(\zeta_2=E[h_2(X,X')^2]\).
The variance formula follows because the two orders are orthogonal, and distinct canonical
pair terms are uncorrelated even when they share one index: condition on that index and use
the zero conditional expectation. For general degree, the canonical projection variances
\(\zeta_s\) obey

$$
\operatorname{Var}(U_n)=\sum_{s=1}^m\frac{\binom ms^2}{\binom ns}\zeta_s.
$$
\alttext(The exact variance is the sum over projection orders s of the square of m choose s, divided by n choose s, times the variance of the order-s canonical projection.)

These are *canonical projection variances*. They must not be substituted unchanged into
formulas that instead use covariances of kernels with a given number of shared arguments.
For fixed \(m\), finite second moment and \(\zeta_1>0\), the first term dominates;
the ordinary i.i.d. central limit theorem and a negligible remainder imply

$$
\sqrt n(U_n-\theta)\xrightarrow{d}N(0,m^2\zeta_1).
$$
\alttext(For a fixed-degree non-degenerate kernel, square root n times the centred U-statistic converges to a normal law with variance m squared times zeta one.)

If \(\zeta_1=0\), this argument gives no nontrivial normal approximation. The rate can
change from \(n^{-1/2}\) to \(n^{-1}\), and the limit can be non-normal. A small computed
standard error does not diagnose which regime applies.

>> What the library's finite-sample diagnostics estimate

For a complete average let \(g_i\) be the mean of the \(\binom{n-1}{m-1}\) terms
containing observation \(i\). Every term is counted \(m\) times, so
\(n^{-1}\sum_i g_i=U_n\). The existing result fields use

$$
\widehat\zeta_1=\frac1{n-1}\sum_i(g_i-U_n)^2,
\qquad\widehat V_{\rm projection}=\frac{m^2}{n}\widehat\zeta_1.
$$
\alttext(The empirical first-projection variance is the sum of squared differences between conditional means and U, divided by n minus one. The legacy sampling-variance estimate multiplies it by m squared over n.)

This is a leading-order estimate, not an exact finite-sample variance. `analyze()` also
computes the complete-fit delete-one values without reevaluating the kernel. Splitting the
complete sum into terms that contain \(i\) and terms that do not gives

$$
U_{(-i)}=\frac{nU_n-mg_i}{n-m},\qquad
\widehat V_{\rm JK}=\frac{n-1}{n}\sum_i(U_{(-i)}-U_n)^2
=\left(\frac{n-1}{n-m}\right)^2\widehat V_{\rm projection}.
$$
\alttext(The statistic with observation i deleted equals n U minus m g i, divided by n minus m. The delete-one jackknife variance is n minus one over n times the sum of squared deleted-value deviations, or the projection estimate multiplied by the square of n minus one over n minus m.)

The pseudo-value is \(P_i=nU_n-(n-1)U_{(-i)}\). Its average equals \(U_n\);
centred pseudo-values show which observations change the estimator most.
The jackknife is still a sampling-variance estimate, with its own non-degeneracy requirements.
It does not rescue a degenerate kernel. If \(n=m\), delete-one values do not exist and are
reported as NaN, even though the legacy projection estimate is zero. `projections` exposes
all these intermediate quantities.

>> Incomplete averages: sampling variability plus computational variability

With `max_terms=B>0`, the library draws \(B\) uniform \(m\)-subsets. Indices are distinct
within a draw, but the same subset can be drawn again. Conditional on the fixed data the draws
are independent. Let \(H\) be one uniformly selected subset's kernel value, and let
\(v_X=\operatorname{Var}(H\mid X_1,\ldots,X_n)\). Then

$$
E[U_{n,B}\mid X]=U_n,\qquad
\operatorname{Var}(U_{n,B}\mid X)=\frac{v_X}{B},
\qquad
\operatorname{Var}(U_{n,B})=\operatorname{Var}(U_n)+\frac{E[v_X]}{B}.
$$
\alttext(The conditional mean of an incomplete average is the complete average. Its conditional Monte Carlo variance is v X over B. Its unconditional variance adds that expected Monte Carlo variance to the complete statistic's sampling variance.)

Equivalently, \(\operatorname{Var}(U_{n,B})=(1-1/B)\operatorname{Var}(U_n)+
\operatorname{Var}(h(X_1,\ldots,X_m))/B\). The conditional Monte Carlo standard error is
estimated from the sampled kernel values, using their sample variance divided by \(B\).
It is unavailable from one draw. It does not describe how the underlying data sample varies.
No finite-population correction applies: subset *draws* are with replacement, even when
\(B\) exceeds the number of distinct subsets.

The legacy incomplete projection fields use noisy inclusion averages, sometimes with uncovered
observations. They are not a calibrated total standard error. `analyze()` reports coverage,
conditional Monte Carlo variance and unavailable jackknife values explicitly. `incomplete_path()`
checks computational RMSE against a complete reference; it is intended for samples where that
reference is tractable.

For a fixed non-degenerate kernel, typically \(B\gg n\) is needed to make computational
variance negligible relative to the \(1/n\) sampling variance. For a degree-two degenerate
kernel the corresponding requirement can be \(B\gg n^2\). Merely letting \(B\) grow
does not give the complete estimator's limiting law. Subbagging and some random-forest
constructions can be analysed as growing-degree U-statistics, but their rates, learner
randomness and regularity assumptions require separate results; this report makes no
automatic infinite-order normality claim.

Enumeration takes \(\binom nm\) kernel calls; an incomplete average takes exactly \(B\).
Bookkeeping costs \(O(m)\) per call and \(O(n+m)\) working memory. Complete enumeration
has no automatic budget cap; an unrepresentable term count is rejected before evaluation.
`term_details()` additionally stores \(O(Bm)\) or \(O(\binom nm m)\) output, so use it
for small examples. Seeded draws reproduce within the same standard-library implementation.

> Simple examples

>> Three observations: see every pair before averaging

For \(x=(1,2,4)\), the variance-kernel values are \(1/2,9/2,2\), hence
\(U=(1/2+9/2+2)/3=7/3\). The ordinary centred sum of squares is \(14/3\), which
also gives \(s^2=(14/3)/(3-1)=7/3\). These are just three *dependent* pair values;
we will not treat them as three independent measurements.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/u_statistic_terms.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const dm::stats::SymmetricKernel kernel=[](const auto& x){return 0.5*(x[0]-x[1])*(x[0]-x[1]);};
  const dm::stats::UStatistic u(2);
  const auto terms=u.term_details({1,2,4},kernel);
  const auto fit=u.run({1,2,4},kernel);
  auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot(terms.numeric_column("kernel").values(),{"(1,2)","(1,4)","(2,4)"});
  plot.abline_h(fit.estimate,{90,90,90}).title("Three pair values; their mean is 7/3")
      .x_label("Pair of observed values").y_label("Half the squared difference").y_limits(0,5.5);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  plot.size(780,450).save_svg("build/u_statistic_terms.svg");
  std::print("U = {:.6f}, {} terms.\n\n{}\n",fit.estimate,fit.num_terms,terms.to_md(3));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
U = 2.333333, 3 terms.

| term | i1 | i2 | x1 | x2 | kernel |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 1 | 0 | 1 | 1 | 2 | 0.5 |
| 2 | 0 | 2 | 1 | 4 | 4.5 |
| 3 | 1 | 2 | 2 | 4 | 2.0 |

\image(figures/u_statistic_terms.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The complete degree-two average on three observations. Indices in the table are zero-based; the chart labels show observed values. The grey line is their average.)
\alttext(Three green bars labelled pairs 1 and 2, 1 and 4, and 2 and 4 have heights 0.5, 4.5 and 2. A horizontal grey line marks their mean, about 2.333.)

>> The inclusion means and the delete-one correction

The inclusion means are \(g=(5/2,5/4,13/4)\). Subtracting \(U=7/3\) gives
\((1/6,-13/12,11/12)\), so \(\widehat\zeta_1=49/48\) and the legacy projection
variance is \(49/36\), with standard error \(7/6\).
Deleting the three observations instead gives \(U_{(-i)}=(2,9/2,1/2)\).
Their jackknife variance is \(49/9\), with standard error \(7/3\): the finite-sample
correction is a factor of four in variance here. Neither estimate is an exact population
variance inferred from just three observations.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/u_statistic_projections.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto fit=dm::stats::UStatistic(2).analyze({1,2,4},
      [](const auto& x){return 0.5*(x[0]-x[1])*(x[0]-x[1]);});
  auto inclusion=dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot(fit.projections.numeric_column("conditional_mean").values(),{"1","2","4"});
  auto deleted=dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot(fit.projections.numeric_column("leave_one_out").values(),{"1","2","4"});
  inclusion.abline_h(fit.result.estimate,{90,90,90}).title("Terms containing each observation")
           .x_label("Observation value").y_label("Conditional mean g_i").y_limits(0,5);
  deleted.abline_h(fit.result.estimate,{90,90,90}).title("Average after deleting each observation")
         .x_label("Deleted value").y_label("Delete-one U").y_limits(0,5);
  auto layout=dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2); layout.add(inclusion).add(deleted);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  layout.size(1100,490).fit_panels().save_svg("build/u_statistic_projections.svg");
  std::print("{}\n\n{}\n",fit.projections.to_md(6),
      fit.summary().select({"estimate","projection_se","jackknife_se","conditional_mc_se"}).to_md(6));
}
```
