>> A four-point sampling law with a genuinely degenerate kernel

Let \(X\) be equally likely to be \(-1\) or \(+1\), and take \(h(x,y)=xy\).
Then \(\theta=(EX)^2=0\) and \(h_1(x)=xEX-\theta=0\) for both population values.
If \(K\) of \(n\) observations equal \(+1\), then \(S=2K-n\) and

$$
U_n=\frac{S^2-n}{n(n-1)},\qquad K\sim\operatorname{Binomial}(n,1/2),
\qquad E[U_n]=0,\quad \operatorname{Var}(U_n)=\frac2{n(n-1)}.
$$
\alttext(The product-kernel U-statistic is S squared minus n divided by n times n minus one, where S is twice the count of plus-one observations minus n. Its expectation is zero and its exact variance is two over n times n minus one.)

For \(n=4\), \(K=0,1,2,3,4\) has probabilities \((1,4,6,4,1)/16\), and the
corresponding U-values are \((1,0,-1/3,0,1)\). A negative unbiased estimate of the
non-negative target \((EX)^2\) is possible. In the balanced sample \((-1,-1,1,1)\),
all inclusion means equal \(-1/3\), so *both* the projection and jackknife standard errors
are zero, despite the population sampling variance being \(1/6\).

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/u_statistic_degenerate.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const dm::stats::SymmetricKernel product=[](const auto& x){return x[0]*x[1];};
  const dm::stats::UStatistic u(2);
  const auto law=u.two_point_law(4,-1,1,0.5,product);
  const auto balanced=u.analyze({-1,-1,1,1},product);
  auto mass=dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot(law.distribution.numeric_column("probability").values(),{"0","1","2","3","4"});
  mass.title("Exact probabilities of the count K").x_label("Number of +1 observations").y_label("Probability").y_limits(0,0.5);
  auto value=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  value.points(law.distribution.numeric_column("high_count").values(),law.distribution.numeric_column("estimate").values(),"",{37,99,235},6)
       .abline_h(0,{90,90,90}).title("U conditional on the count K")
       .x_label("Number of +1 observations").y_label("Product-kernel U").y_limits(-0.5,1.3);
  auto layout=dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2); layout.add(mass).add(value);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  layout.size(1100,490).fit_panels().save_svg("build/u_statistic_degenerate.svg");
  std::print("Target {:.6f}; exact sampling variance {:.6f}; first-order variance {:.6f}.\n\n{}\n\n{}\n",
      law.target,law.variance,law.first_order_variance,law.distribution.to_md(6),
      balanced.summary().select({"estimate","projection_se","jackknife_se"}).to_md(6));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Target 0.000000; exact sampling variance 0.166667; first-order variance 0.000000.

| high_count | probability | estimate |
| ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0 | 0.0625 | 1.000000 |
| 1 | 0.2500 | 0.000000 |
| 2 | 0.3750 | -0.333333 |
| 3 | 0.2500 | 0.000000 |
| 4 | 0.0625 | 1.000000 |


| estimate | projection_se | jackknife_se |
| ---: | ---: | ---: |
