\image(figures/u_statistic_degenerate.svg)
// result_end
\caption(An exact finite-sample law, not a simulation. The balanced sample has zero estimated standard errors even though repeated i.i.d. sampling has nonzero variance.)
\alttext(Left: binomial probabilities at counts zero through four are 0.0625, 0.25, 0.375, 0.25 and 0.0625. Right: U-values are 1, 0, minus one third, 0 and 1; the distribution is not normal.)

Indeed \(S/\sqrt n\) tends to a standard normal \(Z\), while \(\sum X_i^2=n\).
It follows that \(nU_n\) tends to \(Z^2-1\), a centred chi-square law, rather than
a normal distribution. For incomplete sampling in this example, \(\operatorname{Var}(h)=1\),
so its unconditional variance is exactly
\((1-1/B)2/[n(n-1)]+1/B\). This makes the \(B\gg n^2\) requirement concrete.

> Checking the theory

>> Exact sampling moments in two population regimes

For the same product kernel under a general population mean \(\mu\) and variance
\(\sigma^2\), \(h_1(x)=\mu(x-\mu)\) and
\(h_2(x,y)=(x-\mu)(y-\mu)\). Thus

$$
\theta=\mu^2,\qquad
\operatorname{Var}(U_n)=\frac{4\mu^2\sigma^2}{n}+\frac{2\sigma^4}{n(n-1)}.
$$
\alttext(The target is the squared population mean. The exact product-kernel variance is four times mu squared times sigma squared divided by n, plus twice sigma to the fourth divided by n times n minus one.)

On \(\{-1,+1\}\), probability \(p=3/4\) for \(+1\) gives
\(\mu=1/2,\sigma^2=3/4\), so \(n\operatorname{Var}(U_n)\to3/4\).
At \(p=1/2\) the first projection vanishes and \(n^2\operatorname{Var}(U_n)\to2\).
`two_point_law()` computes these laws from binomial and hypergeometric weights: a tuple's
number of high values conditional on the sample count is hypergeometric. It evaluates only
\(m+1\) kernel compositions and does not enumerate \(2^n\) ordered samples.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/u_statistic_rates.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const dm::stats::SymmetricKernel product=[](const auto& x){return x[0]*x[1];};
  std::vector<double> n,logn,regular_scaled,degenerate_scaled,regular_error,degenerate_error;
  for (std::size_t size : {4u,8u,16u,32u,64u,128u}) {
    const auto regular=dm::stats::UStatistic(2).two_point_law(size,-1,1,0.75,product);
    const auto degenerate=dm::stats::UStatistic(2).two_point_law(size,-1,1,0.5,product);
    const double a=0.75/size+1.125/(size*(size-1)),b=2.0/(size*(size-1));
    n.push_back(size); logn.push_back(std::log2(size));
    regular_scaled.push_back(size*regular.variance); degenerate_scaled.push_back(size*size*degenerate.variance);
    regular_error.push_back(std::abs(regular.variance-a)); degenerate_error.push_back(std::abs(degenerate.variance-b));
  }
  auto regular=dm::graphics::RPlot::create(),degenerate=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  regular.lines(logn,regular_scaled,"",{37,99,235}).points(logn,regular_scaled,"",{37,99,235},4)
         .abline_h(0.75,{90,90,90}).title("Non-degenerate: n times variance")
         .x_label("log2(n)").y_label("n Var(U)").y_limits(0.65,1.3);
  degenerate.lines(logn,degenerate_scaled,"",{220,38,38}).points(logn,degenerate_scaled,"",{220,38,38},4)
            .abline_h(2,{90,90,90}).title("Degenerate: n squared times variance")
            .x_label("log2(n)").y_label("n squared Var(U)").y_limits(1.8,2.9);
  auto layout=dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2); layout.add(regular).add(degenerate);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  layout.size(1100,490).fit_panels().save_svg("build/u_statistic_rates.svg");
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame table;
  table.add_column("n",n); table.add_column("n Var regular",regular_scaled); table.add_column("n2 Var degenerate",degenerate_scaled);
  table.add_column("regular formula error",regular_error); table.add_column("degenerate formula error",degenerate_error);
  std::print("{}\n",table.to_md(12));
}
```
