// result_end
\caption(Exact finite sums agree with the full variance formulas in both regimes. The grey lines are the limits 3/4 and 2; the different normalisations are essential.)
\alttext(Two panels show decreasing scaled variances against log base two of sample size. The non-degenerate sequence approaches 0.75 after multiplication by n. The degenerate sequence approaches 2 after multiplication by n squared.)

> A dataset example: iris sepal lengths

>> Describe the sample before attaching inferential language

`dm::datasets::iris()` contains 150 flowers, deliberately balanced at 50 per species.
We analyse sepal length in centimetres, pooled and within each species. Pooled variance
mixes between-species and within-species variation; it is not a measure of variation within
a typical species. No missing values occur in the selected column.

The U estimates below are descriptive summaries of this catalogue. Their unbiasedness and
sampling-error interpretations refer to a hypothetical i.i.d. population model; the balanced
species design is not an i.i.d. random sample from an unspecified iris population. We display
jackknife standard errors as method diagnostics, without claiming calibrated biological
confidence intervals.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto iris=dm::datasets::iris();
  const dm::stats::SymmetricKernel variance=[](const auto& x){return 0.5*(x[0]-x[1])*(x[0]-x[1]);};
  const dm::stats::SymmetricKernel gini=[](const auto& x){return std::abs(x[0]-x[1]);};
  std::vector<std::string> groups; std::vector<double> n,mean,var,gmd,var_se,gmd_se;
  for (const std::string group : {"All","setosa","versicolor","virginica"}) {
    const auto rows=group=="All" ? iris : iris.filter([&](const auto& r){return r.get_string("Species")==group;});
    const auto x=rows.numeric_column("Sepal.Length").values();
    const auto a=dm::stats::UStatistic(2).analyze(x,variance),b=dm::stats::UStatistic(2).analyze(x,gini);
    groups.push_back(group); n.push_back(x.size()); mean.push_back(dm::mean(dm::c(x)));
    var.push_back(a.result.estimate); gmd.push_back(b.result.estimate);
    var_se.push_back(std::sqrt(a.jackknife_variance)); gmd_se.push_back(std::sqrt(b.jackknife_variance));
  }
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame table;
  table.add_column("group",groups); table.add_column("n",n); table.add_column("mean cm",mean);
  table.add_column("variance cm2",var); table.add_column("GMD cm",gmd);
  table.add_column("variance JK SE",var_se); table.add_column("GMD JK SE",gmd_se);
  std::print("{}\n",table.to_md(6));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| group | n | mean cm | variance cm2 | GMD cm | variance JK SE | GMD JK SE |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| All | 150 | 5.843333 | 0.685694 | 0.946192 | 0.067544 | 0.047651 |
| setosa | 50 | 5.006000 | 0.124249 | 0.400408 | 0.023305 | 0.041209 |
| versicolor | 50 | 5.936000 | 0.266433 | 0.591837 | 0.045993 | 0.054990 |
| virginica | 50 | 6.588000 | 0.404343 | 0.717061 | 0.081537 | 0.076596 |
// result_end
\caption(Complete pair averages and illustrative delete-one standard errors. Variance and its SE have squared-centimetre units; Gini mean difference and its SE have centimetre units.)

>> Which observations move the two scale summaries?

The centred pseudo-values below use every pair, with \(\binom{150}{2}=11175\) terms
per kernel. Repeated sepal lengths have identical diagnostic values, so plotted points
overlap. The variance kernel's influence grows quadratically far from the centre, while
the Gini kernel grows linearly in the tails. The two vertical scales have different units.

```{cpp, file="figures/u_statistic_iris_influence.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto iris=dm::datasets::iris();
  const auto x=iris.numeric_column("Sepal.Length").values();
  const std::vector<dm::stats::SymmetricKernel> kernels{
    [](const auto& t){return 0.5*(t[0]-t[1])*(t[0]-t[1]);},
    [](const auto& t){return std::abs(t[0]-t[1]);}};
  auto layout=dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2);
  for (std::size_t k=0;k<kernels.size();++k) {
    const auto fit=dm::stats::UStatistic(2).analyze(x,kernels[k]);
    auto influence=fit.projections.numeric_column("pseudo_value").values();
    for (auto& value:influence) value-=fit.result.estimate;
    auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    plot.points(x,influence,"",k==0 ? dm::RGB{37,99,235} : dm::RGB{220,38,38},4)
        .abline_h(0,{90,90,90}).title(k==0 ? "Variance kernel" : "Gini mean-difference kernel")
        .x_label("Sepal length (cm)").y_label(k==0 ? "Centred pseudo-value (cm2)" : "Centred pseudo-value (cm)");
    layout.add(plot);
  }
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  layout.size(1100,500).fit_panels().save_svg("build/u_statistic_iris_influence.svg");
}
```
// result_begin:
// \image(figures/u_statistic_iris_influence.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Centred delete-one pseudo-values for the pooled sample. These are finite-sample diagnostics, not independent extra observations or a guarantee of outlier resistance.)
\alttext(Two scatter plots show centred pseudo-values against sepal length. The variance curve is U-shaped with stronger positive effects at the extremes. The Gini curve is flatter near the centre and grows more nearly linearly towards the tails.)

>> Check incomplete averaging with repeated seeds

The complete variance U-statistic is cheap enough here to provide an exact computational
reference. For each budget we run 256 seeded incomplete averages on the *same 150 flowers*.
Their RMSE around the complete value should follow \(\sqrt{v_X/B}\). This checks variability
from subset selection; the flowers themselves are not resampled. Budgets share seeds across
rows, so the plotted budget-wise errors are paired, not independent experiments.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/u_statistic_incomplete.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto iris=dm::datasets::iris();
  const auto x=iris.numeric_column("Sepal.Length").values();
  const auto path=dm::stats::UStatistic(2,{0,2024}).incomplete_path(x,
      [](const auto& t){return 0.5*(t[0]-t[1])*(t[0]-t[1]);},{10,30,100,300,1000,3000},256);
  std::vector<double> inverse_root,log_budget;
  for (double b:path.numeric_column("terms").values()) {inverse_root.push_back(1/std::sqrt(b)); log_budget.push_back(std::log10(b));}
  auto error=dm::graphics::RPlot::create(),estimate=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  error.lines(inverse_root,path.numeric_column("expected_rmse").values(),"Predicted RMSE",{90,90,90})
       .points(inverse_root,path.numeric_column("rmse_to_complete").values(),"Observed RMSE",{37,99,235},5)
       .title("Conditional error follows B to -1/2").x_label("1 / sqrt(B)").y_label("RMSE to complete U (cm2)").y_limits(0,0.5);
  const double complete=path.double_at("complete_estimate",0);
  estimate.lines({log_budget.front(),log_budget.back()},{complete,complete},"Complete",{90,90,90})
          .points(log_budget,path.numeric_column("mean_estimate").values(),"Mean estimate",{37,99,235},5)
          .title("Mean over 256 incomplete runs").x_label("log10(B)").y_label("Variance estimate (cm2)").y_limits(0.5,0.9);
  auto layout=dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2); layout.add(error).add(estimate);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  layout.size(1100,500).fit_panels().save_svg("build/u_statistic_incomplete.svg");
  std::print("{}\n",path.select({"terms","mean_estimate","bias_to_complete","rmse_to_complete","expected_rmse"}).to_md(6));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| terms | mean_estimate | bias_to_complete | rmse_to_complete | expected_rmse |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 10 | 0.721953 | 0.036260 | 0.287957 | 0.282313 |
| 30 | 0.689680 | 0.003986 | 0.160814 | 0.162994 |
| 100 | 0.691124 | 0.005431 | 0.092504 | 0.089275 |
| 300 | 0.689023 | 0.003330 | 0.055332 | 0.051543 |
| 1000 | 0.687602 | 0.001908 | 0.029525 | 0.028231 |
| 3000 | 0.685448 | -0.000245 | 0.016916 | 0.016299 |

\image(figures/u_statistic_incomplete.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Observed computational RMSE versus its conditional prediction, and the average incomplete estimate. A single seeded path need not approach the target monotonically; the variance law is the useful check.)
\alttext(Left: observed RMSE markers lie close to a straight predicted line when plotted against one over square root of the number of subset draws. Right: mean variance estimates over 256 runs lie near a horizontal complete-average reference.)

The table's `bias_to_complete` is the error of a finite average over seeds, not a claim
that uniform subset sampling is biased. At the smallest budget its observed mean can still
sit visibly above the target. The RMSE comparison concerns the distribution across repeated
runs, not monotonic improvement along one random sequence.

For independent validation, R 4.5.2 `var`, pairwise absolute differences and explicit
delete-one refits reproduce all eight iris summaries and their jackknife standard errors.
R `dbinom` and independent integer-binomial calculations reproduce all 12 count laws.
NumPy also reconstructs every inclusion mean and pseudo-value, checks 4440 recorded random
tuples against their indices, and independently aggregates the 1536 incomplete runs into
the displayed conditional-error path. These checks are separate from the closed-form
variance comparisons above.

> Conclusion

A symmetric kernel makes the estimand explicit; distinct-index averaging produces an
unbiased estimate without requiring independent kernel terms. The inclusion means reveal
why those terms are dependent and how delete-one diagnostics can be obtained from a single
complete enumeration. The three-point calculation also shows how large the finite-sample
jackknife correction can be.

The exact product-kernel law supplies a limit to first-order reasoning: a balanced sample
can report zero projection and jackknife uncertainty even though repeated sampling has
positive variance and a non-normal limit. Incomplete averaging introduces a second source
of variability with a different experimental interpretation. On the fixed iris sample it
follows the conditional \(B^{-1/2}\) law; it cannot be substituted for uncertainty about
the population or used to justify unrestricted growing-degree normal approximations.

> References

- W. Hoeffding (1948). *A class of statistics with asymptotically normal distribution*.
  Annals of Mathematical Statistics 19(3), 293–325.
  [Original paper|https://doi.org/10.1214/aoms/1177730196].
- G. Blom (1976). *Some properties of incomplete U-statistics*. Biometrika 63(3), 573–580.
  [Original paper|https://doi.org/10.1093/biomet/63.3.573].
- L. Mentch and G. Hooker (2016). *Quantifying Uncertainty in Random Forests via Confidence Intervals and Hypothesis Tests*.
  Journal of Machine Learning Research 17(26), 1–41.
  [Paper and assumptions for subsampled ensembles|https://www.jmlr.org/papers/v17/14-168.html].
